WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:11.070
The polls tell us that Americans do not want
the Supreme Court to overturn its 1973 Roe

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.030
v. Wade ruling on abortion.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:17.980
In a Gallup question from July, 64 percent,
did not want the decision overturned.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.940
New polls from Fox News and the Kaiser Family
Foundation produced similar results.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.500
That said, most Americans believe that abortion
should be legal only under certain circumstances,

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:32.529
and they are willing to put considerable restrictions
on the procedure.

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:37.520
In Gallup’s polling on the issue, for example,
around 60 percent say it should be possible

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.780
for a woman to obtain a legal abortion in the first three months of a pregnancy.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.010
That drops sharply to around 30 percent when
people are asked about abortions in the second

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:51.190
trimester, and only around 10 percent when
pollsters ask about the third trimester.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:55.699
In Gallup’s latest poll from May this year,
48 percent of people said they considered

00:00:55.699 --> 00:01:01.020
themselves to be pro-life, and an identical
48 percent said they were pro-choice.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.390
The split reflects Americans’ beliefs in
the sanctity of life and the importance of

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.620
individual choice.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.420
Do you think roe v. Wade should be overturned? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.740
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.320
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

